{
  "abstract": "Paper 40 attaches to a putative rational Diophantine septuple $A$ the triangle-free graph $G(A)$ whose edges $pq$ mark regular quintuples $A\\setminus\\{x_p,x_q\\}$. Graphs with a vertex of degree at least four, and cycles of maximum degree two, are treated in papers 45, 46, 49, 52 and 55. The 48 graphs of maximum degree three are not. We prove that $G(A)$ never contains $K_{2,3}$. More precisely, no seven distinct nonzero elements of a field of characteristic 0 without $\\sqrt2$ satisfy the six $K_{2,3}$ windows. Square conditions are not used, so zero radicands are covered. The mechanism is an explicit identity: the two vertices $s,t$ outside the $K_{2,3}$ satisfy $x_s^2x_t^2(x_s-x_t)^2=2(x_sx_t+1)^2$. The three shared neighbours force three quadratics with the same two roots, and an msolve characteristic-0 emptiness certificate in four leading-coefficient charts shows the identity on the resulting curve. We found the identity from exact $\\mathbb F_p$ counts, which are nonzero exactly when $(2/p)=1$ at eleven primes, and from two exact slices whose 48 points all lie over fields containing $\\sqrt2$. It holds at all 5232 enumerated $\\mathbb F_p$-points. It also holds on the six exact octic orbits of paper 55's row 53, computed there with an independent system, which explains why those orbits are never rational. The theorem excludes 28 of the 107 graphs, ten of them for the first time. We add an exact $\\mathbb F_p$ census of the maximum-degree-three graphs, at the evidence level: two strata of excess dimension inherited from the $C_6+K_1$ porism, mod-113 genus lower bounds for several curve strata, and the list of graphs that are $\\mathbb F_p$-empty or carry isolated points. No septuple is constructed, and nonexistence is not claimed beyond the theorem. The certificate rests on msolve's multimodular computation.",
